format: meaning/draft-1
id: delivery
name: Software delivery
description: >-
  How software work moves from a backlog to a running environment: work
  items, iterations, releases, deployments and the measures of that flow.
license: CC0-1.0
concepts:
  - id: work-item
    kind: entity
    extends: task
    labels:
      en: Work item
    synonyms:
      en: [backlog item]
    description: >-
      A task tracked on a delivery backlog. An epic, a user story, a delivery
      task, a bug, a spike, and a technical-debt item are kinds of work item.
      The general task is any assignable piece of work, including work that
      is not on a delivery backlog. A technical step under a story is a
      delivery task, not a second meaning of task. A work item is not a
      support ticket. A requirement is a representation of a need. The work
      item is delivery work that may satisfy that need, and a user story's
      text often is that representation. When both are recorded, requirement
      work item links them.

  - id: work-item-status
    kind: attribute
    of: work-item
    labels:
      en: Work item status
    description: >-
      Where this work item sits in the team's workflow. The state names
      belong to the team. Status is not priority, and it is not which sprint
      the item is in.

  - id: work-item-priority
    kind: attribute
    of: work-item
    labels:
      en: Work item priority
    description: >-
      The order in which a delivery team intends to pick up this work item
      among the others. It is a planning decision. It is not bug severity,
      which grades a flaw, and it is not requirement priority, which grades
      a need. A bug is a work item, so it can carry a severity and this
      priority at the same time.

  - id: work-item-story-points
    kind: attribute
    of: work-item
    labels:
      en: Work item story points
    synonyms:
      en: [story points, story point estimate]
    description: >-
      A relative estimate of size for a work item. The Scrum Guide does not
      define story points. Teams most often point a user story, and
      sometimes a bug or a spike. The points are not hours, and a total is
      not comparable across teams as a unit of time. An epic is usually left
      unpointed, because its size is the items under it. Velocity is the sum
      of points completed in one sprint. It is not this attribute on a
      single item.
    unit: story points

  - id: work-item-parent
    kind: attribute
    of: work-item
    labels:
      en: Work item parent
    synonyms:
      en: [parent work item]
    description: >-
      A larger work item this one is split from, such as the epic of a user
      story. The parent is not the project and not the sprint.
    values-of: work-item

  - id: work-item-sprint
    kind: attribute
    of: work-item
    labels:
      en: Work item sprint
    description: >-
      A sprint this work item is taken into. An item can move to a later
      sprint; the sprint is not part of the item's identity. A team that
      does not use sprints leaves this empty.
    values-of: sprint

  - id: epic
    kind: entity
    extends: work-item
    labels:
      en: Epic
    description: >-
      A work item larger than one delivery increment, split into smaller
      work items such as user stories. The Scrum Guide does not define an
      epic. Some writers call an epic a large user story before it is split.
      In this graph the epic is the parent item, so it is not recorded as a
      user story. It is not a sprint. A sprint is a time box, and an epic
      can stay open across several of them. It is not a release. A release
      is a version made available for use, and it may contain work from
      several epics or from only part of one.

  - id: user-story
    kind: entity
    extends: work-item
    labels:
      en: User story
    synonyms:
      en: [story]
    description: >-
      A work item for a small outcome valuable to a user, often told as who
      wants what and why. It is not the epic it was split from. A technical
      step under the story is a delivery task. It is not a use case. A use
      case narrates how an actor reaches a goal, with a main flow and
      alternatives, and is not itself a backlog item. The story's text is
      often a usable representation of a stakeholder need. In this graph the
      requirement is that representation when it is recorded on its own, and
      this story is the delivery item. Link them with requirement work item
      when both exist. The story is not an acceptance criterion. A criterion
      is a condition for accepting the story, a requirement, or a service.

  - id: delivery-task
    kind: entity
    extends: work-item
    labels:
      en: Delivery task
    synonyms:
      en: [implementation task, sprint task]
    description: >-
      A work item for a technical step that carries out part of a user story
      or another backlog item. Planning conversations often call this step a
      task. This graph already uses task for any assignable piece of work,
      and a user story and an epic are kinds of task, so this concept is the
      step under a story rather than another name for every task. It does
      not by itself promise an outcome a user receives, so it is not a user
      story. It is not a spike. A spike's result is knowledge, inside the
      spike's own time box.

  - id: bug
    kind: entity
    extends: work-item
    labels:
      en: Bug
    synonyms:
      en: [defect, software defect, software bug]
    description: >-
      A flaw in software, recorded as a work item so it can be corrected.
      Defect is another name for this same flaw. Testing glossaries treat
      bug, defect, and fault as that flaw in a work product. Some
      organizations also use defect for a flaw found after release and bug
      for one found before. That split records when it was found, so both
      words still name this concept. A bug is not an incident. The incident
      is an unplanned interruption of a running service, which a bug may
      cause. Many bugs are found before they harm anyone, and an incident
      can have a cause other than a bug. A bug is not technical debt. Debt
      can meet its specification and still make later change expensive. A
      failure is a loss of ability to operate to specification or to deliver
      the required outcome. The word error is not a synonym: ITIL uses it
      for a flaw or vulnerability that may cause incidents, software testing
      uses it for the human mistake that introduces a defect, and
      dependability writing uses it for a wrong internal state. A known
      error is a problem that has been analysed, not a name for this flaw.

  - id: bug-severity
    kind: attribute
    of: bug
    labels:
      en: Bug severity
    synonyms:
      en: [defect severity]
    description: >-
      How far the software's behavior departs from what was specified or
      expected, from data loss or a crash to a cosmetic miss. It grades the
      flaw. It does not say when a team will fix it; that order is the work
      item's priority. Incident severity grades impairment of a running
      service and is a different attribute. One flaw can have a bug severity
      and, once it hurts a service, an incident severity as well.

  - id: bug-steps-to-reproduce
    kind: attribute
    of: bug
    labels:
      en: Bug steps to reproduce
    synonyms:
      en: [steps to reproduce]
    description: >-
      The sequence a person follows to make the flaw appear. It is evidence
      on the bug, not the severity and not the correction.

  - id: technical-debt-item
    kind: entity
    extends: work-item
    labels:
      en: Technical debt item
    synonyms:
      en: [technical debt, tech debt]
    description: >-
      A work item that records a shortcut, a workaround left in place, or a
      design that has aged, where the cost appears as slower or riskier
      change later. The software may meet its requirements, so this is not a
      bug and not a defect. It is not an incident: nothing has to be failing
      now. It is scheduled with a priority, like any other work item.

  - id: spike
    kind: entity
    extends: work-item
    labels:
      en: Spike
    synonyms:
      en: [research spike]
    description: >-
      A time-boxed work item whose result is knowledge: an answer that
      unblocks an estimate or a design. The practice comes from Extreme
      Programming and is used with Scrum. The Scrum Guide does not define
      it. It does not promise an outcome a user receives, so it is not a
      user story. It is not a bug. The time box belongs to the spike. A
      sprint is the team's iteration and is not another name for a spike.

  - id: sprint
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Sprint
    synonyms:
      en: [scrum sprint]
    description: >-
      A fixed-length iteration in which a team takes on a set of work items.
      In Scrum a sprint lasts at most one month. It has a start day and an
      end day. Work left unfinished stays a work item. It does not become a
      property of the sprint. A sprint may be run inside a project and may
      be recorded with none. The Scrum Guide does not make a sprint a part
      of a project, and it does not define story points or velocity. A
      sprint is not a release and not a deployment.

  - id: sprint-project
    kind: attribute
    of: sprint
    labels:
      en: Sprint project
    description: >-
      The project this sprint is part of, when the team runs it inside a
      project. A sprint can be recorded with no project.
    values-of: project

  - id: sprint-start
    kind: attribute
    of: sprint
    extends: date
    labels:
      en: Sprint start
    description: The calendar day the sprint starts.

  - id: sprint-end
    kind: attribute
    of: sprint
    extends: date
    labels:
      en: Sprint end
    description: The calendar day the sprint ends.

  - id: release
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Release
    synonyms:
      en: [software release]
    description: >-
      A version made available for use. For software, release version names
      that version. A release of a service, with no software version, leaves
      the version empty. The same release can be deployed more than once and
      into more than one environment. A release is not a deployment, not a
      build, and not an environment. It is not a sprint. A feature flag can
      change what users see of a release that is already deployed, without
      making another release.

  - id: release-version
    kind: attribute
    of: release
    labels:
      en: Release version
    description: >-
      The software version this release makes available. The version
      identifies the software; the release is the decision to offer it.
    values-of: software-version

  - id: release-work-item
    kind: attribute
    of: release
    labels:
      en: Release work item
    description: >-
      A work item included in this release. The work item is not the
      release, and a deployment of the release is a further fact.
    values-of: work-item

  - id: deployment
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Deployment
    synonyms:
      en: [software deployment]
    description: >-
      The act of putting a build into an environment. The same release can
      be deployed many times, and a deployment can fail to reach the
      environment while the release still exists. A deployment is not a
      release, not a version, and not the environment it targets. It is not
      a change request. The change request is the proposal, and a deployment
      is one way that proposal is carried out. Putting bytes into an
      environment is not the same as showing new behavior to users. A
      feature flag can do that with no further deployment. Whether the
      deployment reached the environment, and whether it then required
      remediation, is the deployment outcome.

  - id: deployment-environment
    kind: attribute
    of: deployment
    labels:
      en: Deployment environment
    description: >-
      The environment the build is put into. A deployment to staging and a
      deployment to production are different deployments even when they
      carry the same release.
    values-of: environment

  - id: deployment-date
    kind: attribute
    of: deployment
    labels:
      en: Deployment date
    description: >-
      When the deployment was carried out. The record may be a calendar day
      or an instant. It is not the identity of the release and not the
      version number.

  - id: deployment-release
    kind: attribute
    of: deployment
    labels:
      en: Deployment release
    description: >-
      The release this deployment puts into an environment, when the
      deployment is of a named release. Deploying a build that was never
      named as a release leaves this empty.
    values-of: release

  - id: deployment-build
    kind: attribute
    of: deployment
    labels:
      en: Deployment build
    description:       The build that was put into the environment.
    values-of: build

  - id: deployment-outcome
    kind: attribute
    of: deployment
    labels:
      en: Deployment outcome
    description: >-
      What this deployment did: whether it reached the target environment,
      and whether the service then had to be remediated, for example by a
      rollback or a follow-up fix. A deployment that never reaches
      production is not counted in deployment frequency and is not a change
      failure. A deployment that reaches production and degrades the service
      is counted in the frequency and in the change failure rate. The
      outcome of one deployment is not either of those rates.

  - id: deployment-change-request
    kind: attribute
    of: deployment
    labels:
      en: Deployment change request
    description: >-
      The change request this deployment carries out, when one is recorded.
      A deployment can have no change request, and a change can be carried
      out by something other than a deployment.
    values-of: change-request

  - id: pull-request
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Pull request
    synonyms:
      en: [merge request]
    description: >-
      A proposal to bring one or more commits into a line of development. A
      merge request is that same proposal under another tool's name. The
      proposal is not a code review: the review is an examination of it, and
      one proposal can have several reviews. It is not a change request,
      which judges a change to a service, and it is not a deployment.

  - id: pull-request-repository
    kind: attribute
    of: pull-request
    labels:
      en: Pull request repository
    description: The repository whose line of development the proposal targets.
    values-of: repository

  - id: code-review
    kind: entity
    of: pull-request
    labels:
      en: Code review
    description: >-
      An examination of a pull request by a person, recorded as remarks or a
      decision. The review is not the pull request. Several reviews can
      belong to one pull request.

  - id: code-review-pull-request
    kind: attribute
    of: code-review
    labels:
      en: Code review pull request
    description: The pull request this review examines.
    values-of: pull-request

  - id: velocity
    kind: measure
    of: sprint
    labels:
      en: Velocity
    synonyms:
      en: [sprint velocity]
    description: >-
      The sum of story points on the work items a team completed in one
      sprint. The Scrum Guide does not define velocity. It is a planning aid
      for that team. It is not a count of hours, not deployment frequency,
      and not a unit that can be compared across teams as time. Adding the
      velocities of several sprints does not produce a larger velocity. The
      reading belongs to one sprint. Velocity is not a DORA delivery metric.
    unit: story points per sprint
    measure:
      formula: >-
        The sum of the story points of the work items completed in the sprint.
      aggregation: none
      inputs: [work-item-story-points]

  - id: cycle-time
    kind: measure
    labels:
      en: Cycle time
    description: >-
      The elapsed time from when work starts on a work item until that work
      is finished. It is not lead time for changes, which runs from a commit
      until the change is running in production. It is not the wait from a
      request arriving until delivery; that longer interval is sometimes
      also called lead time, and it is not this measure. The length of a
      sprint is not a cycle time.
    measure:
      formula: >-
        For a finished work item, the elapsed time from the start of work
        until the work is finished. A figure for a team over a window is
        recomputed from the items in that window.
      aggregation: none

  - id: deployment-frequency
    kind: measure
    labels:
      en: Deployment frequency
    description: >-
      How often changes are successfully deployed to production, as a count
      per unit of time over a stated window. This is one of DORA's software
      delivery metrics, with lead time for changes, change failure rate, and
      failed deployment recovery time. A deployment that does not reach
      production is not counted. A deployment that reaches production and
      later needs remediation is still counted. The share that needs
      remediation is the change failure rate. One named release can be
      deployed more than once, so this counts deployments. A release to end
      users with no separate deployment, such as turning a feature flag on,
      counts when the team treats that release as the production release,
      and is not counted again when it is the same event as a deployment.
    measure:
      formula: >-
        The number of deployments that reached production in the window,
        divided by the length of the window. A deployment that never reaches
        production is excluded. Recomputed for the window.
      aggregation: none

  - id: lead-time-for-changes
    kind: measure
    labels:
      en: Lead time for changes
    synonyms:
      en: [change lead time]
    description: >-
      How long a change takes from a commit until it is deployed in
      production. This is one of DORA's software delivery metrics. The clock
      ends at the production deployment, not when a feature flag later
      exposes the change and not when a customer first asked for it. Over a
      window, this measure is the median of those durations, which is how
      the Four Keys measurement summarizes them. DORA's survey asks for the
      typical time. A mean of the same durations is a different summary and
      is not this measure. The interval is not cycle time, which starts when
      work starts rather than at the commit, and it is not the time from a
      customer request until delivery. Both of those are sometimes called
      lead time. This concept is only commit to production deployment. It
      is not deployment frequency.
    measure:
      formula: >-
        The median, over changes deployed to production in the window, of the
        elapsed time from the commit until that production deployment.
      aggregation: none

  - id: change-failure-rate
    kind: measure
    labels:
      en: Change failure rate
    description: >-
      The proportion of production deployments, including releases to users
      that the team counts as deployments, that degrade the service and need
      remediation such as a hotfix, a rollback, or a fix forward. A bug
      found before production is outside the rate. So is an incident that a
      deployment did not cause, and a pipeline run that never reached
      production. DORA's later deployment rework rate, the proportion of
      deployments that are unplanned work to fix defects, is not this rate.
      This is one of DORA's software delivery metrics. It is not deployment
      frequency, which is a count of deployments that reached production,
      per unit of time.
    unit: proportion
    measure:
      formula: >-
        The number of production deployments in the window that degrade the
        service and require remediation, divided by the number of production
        deployments in that window. A deployment that never reaches
        production is in neither count. Recomputed for the window.
      aggregation: none
